is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: DNS service (BIND/named) on dns_server. The DNS daemon is up and listening on 10.200.0.2:53, but its configuration is incorrect for client resolution.

Mechanism: host_1_1_1_1 can reach servers by IP (curl http://10.200.0.3 is fast; ICMP OK), but any name-based access hangs/fails because DNS lookups are refused. From host_1_1_1_1, nslookup web_server_0 10.200.0.2 returns “REFUSED,” and ping web_server_0 fails with “Temporary failure in name resolution.” This causes applications to delay/retry DNS until they time out.

Localization: dns_server’s BIND instance. Evidence on the device:
- named is running (/usr/sbin/named -f -u bind) and listening on 10.200.0.2:53 (ss -plntu).
- /etc/bind/named.conf explicitly sets “recursion no;” and there are no internal zones defined (named.conf.local is empty). With recursion disabled and no authoritative zone for internal names (e.g., web_server_0), the server returns REFUSED to clients.

Root cause: BIND on dns_server was configured with recursion disabled and no authoritative internal zones/forwarders, causing it to refuse client queries.

Corrective fix: On dns_server, enable recursion for internal clients and/or define the internal DNS zone. For example, in /etc/bind/named.conf or named.conf.options set “recursion yes;” and add an allow-recursion ACL (e.g., {10.0.0.0/8;};), or create an authoritative zone (e.g., corp.local) with A records for web_server_0–3, dns_server, etc. Optionally add forwarders if using upstream DNS. Reload/restart named (rndc reload or systemctl start/restart bind9). Afterward, verify from host_1_1_1_1 that dig/ping/curl to web_server_0 by name succeeds.
=== END ===